hello again  it's windmill wednesday and i'm trevor  and we're here again outside the  windmill because we're not allowed to go  inside  i thought this time might sort of say  try and tell you a little bit about what  uh how the mill  would have worked so we've got six  floors in the windmill of which  four we can physically visit uh but the  other two  are not allowed to visit too narrow up  there and too dangerous  so we'll start the ground floor so if  you go through the front doors  this is where the grain would have been  brought in from the farms etc  and there would have been a desk and  somebody to to weigh off  the uh the grain as it arrived to make  sure they all weighed correctly in the  sacks  probably as much as 200 pound per sec  one heck of a size uh and then they'll  be hoisted  all through all the floors all the way  to the top of the mill  so on the next floor up is our museum  floor it's the second floor  and uh and we would uh this is where the  miller works it's really the command  center  and he above his head would have been  hanging from the ceiling  an enormous great big what is known as  the giant spur wheel  full of gearing covered in gearing  taking bring all the power from the  sails will be brought down to that  that one wheel now from that stone nuts  would operate  all the other machinery in the mill  including on the floor above it  which was where the stones were located  okay so the uh on on the second floor as  i say there was the three sets of there  was three sets of stones  two were for a million grain and they  were the best stones and they were made  from french burst  the other set of stones uh was made from  derbyshire gritstone and just outside  the middle you'll see one of the stones  that was  the only one that remains and that's one  that's used for the domestic grits don't  want to use from  the byproducts and adding to other  meal to make animal feed  uh above the uh the next floor which is  the highest point we can go to  uh is was known as the pin floor so all  the grain has gone all the way to the  top of the mill above the bin floor  so the grain could be to be stored so  the grain to be fed into the mill  into the bins to feed the stones below  and and the miller below that is  operating  all the machinery from from there  okay so the two floors at the top of the  mill which we can't visit  uh one is where i just said the uh the  grain was stored prior to it  uh being tipped into the bins to start  the process but above that  there's a low ceiling area known as the  dust floor now the dust floor is there  generally just  trying to minimize any debris that could  get in through the cap  some a bird can fly in even a bee could  fly in  that don't any of that getting into the  process so the dust floor  minimizes that risk and then above that  of course we've got the cap  and the cap resembles an upturned boat  or looks like a wagon  and at the rear of that is the fan tail  now the fan tail is designed just to  catch the minimum amount of wind  and freely turn the cap into the wind so  the sails are always  facing the wind if the mill wasn't  working and you walk through the front  doors  you may even hear it creaking like a  haunted house  because the rollers are trying  desperately or the phantom  is trying desperately to turn the  rollers and turn the cap into the wind  well that was just a quick run down as  to how the mill operated such a big mill  and so many flaws it spread out somewhat  that our process  spread out so widely well that's it for  today thanks very much for tuning in and  we'll see you again  next week on windmill wednesday  you

